HDM and Constant Sum Methodology: A Comprehensive Report
Sources: the HDM chapters of A. Cavanaugh's dissertation (Portland State University, 2024); the CSAHP repository (github.com/aaroncav2/CSAHP), including its README and Excel workbooks; and the calculators built in this working thread. This report covers method only, not study findings.
1. Summary
The Hierarchical Decision Model (HDM) turns expert pairwise judgments into weights by evaluating every ordering (permutation) of the items being compared. The spread of the results across orderings is a built-in measure of how consistent the expert was. The constant sum method is the way judgments are collected: the expert splits a fixed total between the two items of each pair. CSAHP, the repository's hybrid tool, combines constant sum input with HDM-style and AHP-style consistency checks.
•	Elicitation: constant sum pairwise comparisons, so the two scores of a pair always add up to the same total (100 in HDM and in the thread's tools, 10 in the CSAHP workbooks).
•	Core calculation: ratios between items, raw weights for every permutation, normalization, then the mean and standard deviation across permutations.
•	Quality checks: HDM inconsistency, AHP consistency ratio, a standard-deviation-based CSAHP score, and a density score; acceptance is commonly judged against 0.10.
•	Group level: average the experts' weights, measure disagreement, and combine perspective and local weights into global weights.
•	Extensions: desirability scoring, composite scores, sensitivity scenarios and optional alternatives.
•	Scale limit: the work grows as n factorial, which is why spreadsheets stop at about nine items per group and why this thread added a script and web form.
2. Context: from AHP to HDM to CSAHP
•	MCDM: multi-criteria decision-making methods break a decision into a hierarchy so that the effect of each criterion on the objective can be seen.
•	AHP (Analytic Hierarchy Process): the most widely used method. Experts compare pairs on Saaty's 1 to 9 scale (with reciprocals), and consistency is checked with an eigenvalue-based consistency ratio. The dissertation notes that Saaty's index only covers about 10 items per group, that spreadsheets approximate the eigenvalue, and that rankings can change depending on how scores are combined.
•	HDM: keeps the hierarchy and pairwise idea but uses constant sum judgments and evaluates all permutations (the orientation matrix). The standard deviation across permutations gives a direct inconsistency measure, and group agreement can be tested the same way.
•	CSAHP: the repository's tool. It is described as a hybrid of AHP and HDM that uses a constant sum scale in an AHP-style matrix, so that an expert's ranking can be validated by either an HDM-type or an AHP-type consistency score.
Terminology used in the repository (HDM term / AHP term):
•	Level 1: Mission / Focus.
•	Level 2: Perspective / Actors.
•	Level 3: Criterion / Objectives.
•	Level 4: Alternative / Scenario.
Benefits claimed for HDM: it captures subjective judgment as numbers, aggregates diverse experts without meetings, allows inconsistency and disagreement to be tested, supports sensitivity testing, and suits new topic areas where regression data does not yet exist.
3. Model structure
•	Mission: the single objective at the top of the hierarchy.
•	Perspectives: affinity categories that group related criteria. They are chosen from an existing framework or by grouping the criteria, and they carry an importance weight toward the mission.
•	Criteria: the items inside each perspective. Each perspective has its own pairwise comparison set.
•	Alternatives (optional): options or scenarios to be scored against the model, for example cost or acquisition choices. In the CSAHP workbooks they are handled in a separate workbook.
•	Skip-level comparisons: the CSAHP tool compares alternatives (Level 4) directly with perspectives (Level 2), so each perspective is evaluated once against the set of alternatives.
•	Group size: pairwise judgments become hard for respondents above about 10 items. Keep each group to nine or fewer where possible, which also keeps the permutation count manageable.
4. The constant sum method
4.1 What it is
For each pair of items the expert divides a fixed total of points between them. A bigger share means the item matters more in that comparison. Because the two scores always add up to the total, the expert enters one number and the other is its complement. Only one value per pair is collected, and the reciprocal is derived.
4.2 Why it is used with HDM
•	It is intuitive: it reads like a percentage, with no reciprocal scale to learn.
•	It forces a trade-off, so no item can be rated highly against every other item.
•	The ratio between two items comes straight from the two shares, which is what HDM's ratio matrix needs.
•	The same input feeds both an HDM-style and an AHP-style calculation.
4.3 The 100-point form (HDM and this thread's tools)
For a pair, an expert gives a and 100 minus a. The ratio of the first item to the second is a divided by (100 minus a). The thread's web form requires a whole number from 1 to 99 so that each pair totals 100 and no ratio is zero or undefined. Ratios can therefore reach 99 to 1, much wider than the 9 to 1 limit of Saaty's scale.
4.4 The 1 to 10 form (CSAHP workbooks)
•	The expert picks n from 1 to 10 and the derived reciprocal is (10 minus n) divided by n.
•	Because the 1 to 10 scale is a proxy for the full AHP range of 1/9 up to 9 (17 distinct values), the repository describes the scale as a proxy model.
•	The repository's grid adds one to the reciprocal scores so that the effective range is wider than 1 to 10 (the README describes an effective range of about 12 and 20 possible grid points). Survey answers are shifted by one before they enter the grid, as a note on the grid sheet explains. The README's grid table lists the exact values.
4.5 The misranking problem it solves
In AHP, averaging the normalized columns can produce a different ranking than a simple raw sum of the same matrix, which could lead a decision maker to the wrong choice. The repository includes a demonstration workbook of this mismatch. Its constant sum grid widens the range of values so that the raw sum never changes the rank order. A 1 to 100 scale also leaves enough room to avoid the problem, and a second demonstration workbook shows this.
4.6 Practical cautions
•	Some survey tools cap the scale at 10. The README advises respondents to ignore the printed numbers and treat the linear-scale boxes as how strongly they favor one item over the other.
•	A coarse 1 to 10 scale limits how finely an expert can express a preference, which is one reason the 100-point form is used in HDM.
•	Every pair must be answered, and extremes (0 or 100) are not allowed because they make ratios infinite.
5. HDM calculation step by step
5.1 Raw and ratio matrices
•	The expert's scores fill the lower triangle of a matrix. The upper triangle is the complement (100 minus the score) and the diagonal is zero. This is the Raw matrix.
•	The Ratio matrix divides each raw cell by its mirror cell:
R_{ij} = \frac{\mathrm{Raw}_{ij}}{\mathrm{Raw}_{ji}}, \qquad R_{ii} = 1
5.2 Orientations and reference values
For each item k the ratio matrix is re-oriented so that k comes first (the r1, r2, ... blocks in the workbooks). The reference value comparing item k with item j is the average over all rows i of R(i,k) divided by R(i,j). These values form the Vlookup table that every permutation draws from.
5.3 Permutations and raw weights
For each of the n! orderings (a1, a2, ..., an):
•	the adjacent values are the reference values for each neighboring pair in the ordering;
•	the last item's raw weight is 1, and each earlier item's raw weight is its adjacent value times the weight of the item after it (running products from the end);
•	the raw weights are added up and each is divided by the sum, so every ordering produces weights that total 1 (normalization).
5.4 Re-aligning and summarizing
The normalized weights are mapped back to the original item letters (the rotation step). Over all orderings the model reports, per item, the average score (the item's weight, summing to 1) and the standard deviation.
5.5 The computational limit
The number of orderings is n factorial: 4 gives 24, 5 gives 120, 6 gives 720, 7 gives 5,040, 8 gives 40,320, 9 gives 362,880, 10 gives 3,628,800 and 12 gives 479,001,600. A spreadsheet needs about one row per ordering and Excel allows 1,048,576 rows, so the repository states the practical Excel limit as 9 factorial.
6. Consistency and quality checks
6.1 Consistency in principle
•	Ordinal: if A is preferred to B and B to C, then A must be preferred to C.
•	Cardinal: proportions must carry through. If A is twice B and B is three times C, then A must be six times C.
•	HDM treats inconsistency as the variation in the distribution of relative values across permutations. Perfectly proportional judgments give the same weights in every ordering and an inconsistency of 0.
6.2 HDM inconsistency
•	Original measure: the mean of the item standard deviations.
•	RSV measure: the square root of the sum of the squared standard deviations (Root Sum of Variance), looked up in a table that depends on the number of items. The workbooks hold that table for 3 to 12 items, and the script and web form report which table percentiles bracket the value.
•	Threshold: an expert whose inconsistency exceeds 0.10 retakes the questionnaire, and is removed if still above 0.10. A value of 0.04 or lower is a stretch goal. If many experts exceed the limit, cluster analysis of decision makers is suggested instead of discarding data.
6.3 AHP consistency ratio (CSAHP workbooks)
The workbook computes the principal eigenvalue estimate (the vector average) and then:
CI = \frac{\lambda_{max} - n}{n - 1}, \qquad CR = \frac{CI}{RI}
The random index values on its scale reference sheet are 0.90 for 4 items, 1.12 for 5, 1.24 for 6, 1.32 for 7, 1.41 for 8, 1.46 for 9, 1.49 for 10 and 1.51 for 11. A note on the sheet says 11 items could not be used in the original Saaty index, so the value was extended.
6.4 CSAHP inconsistency (standard deviation based)
The repository adds a matrix-based score built from standard deviations. As read from the workbook formulas, one version averages the column standard deviations of the normalized matrix, and another takes the standard deviation of the ratioed matrix divided by the number of comparisons, n times (n minus 1). The README's acceptance rule is to accept the user's ranking as valid if either CSAHP consistency score falls below 0.10.
6.5 Density
Density measures how much differentiation the expert expressed. The workbook counts the neutral ('ones') cells on one side of the matrix and divides by the number of comparisons per side, n(n minus 1) divided by 2. A score above 0.5 means little differentiation (high density). The README says this is not necessarily bad: it can mean the model contains several vital activities that cannot easily be separated. The neutral-value threshold is customizable.
6.6 Focus charts
Focus charts show the normalized matrix averages and standard deviations for each item. They are meant as a deep-dive reference when debating one implementation against another.
7. Group aggregation and disagreement
•	Cumulative table: each expert contributes one row (the average score of every item plus that expert's inconsistency). The table adds Mean, Minimum, Maximum and Std. Deviation rows. The Mean row is the group's weight for each item.
•	Disagreement (dissertation definition): group disagreement is expressed through standard deviation. The index is the average of the standard deviations of the group's weights (the Std. Deviation row). If it exceeds 0.10 the experts are considered to disagree. The content validity ratio and the intraclass correlation coefficient are named as alternatives. There is no objective standard for disagreement, so a high value does not by itself mean the model is wrong.
•	Difference in this thread's web form: by instruction, the form currently calculates Disagreement as the average of the respondents' Inconsistency values. That is a different quantity from the dissertation's definition, so the two will not match. See section 15.
8. Weights across the hierarchy
•	Perspective value: the Mean row of the perspectives comparison, from the experts' pairwise judgments of the perspectives against each other.
•	Local weight: a criterion's Mean within its own perspective. The local weights of one perspective sum to 1, so across all perspectives they sum to the number of perspectives.
•	Global weight: perspective value times local weight. The global weights of all criteria sum to 1.
•	CSAHP Step 2: the repository's desirability workbook builds global weights from the perspective weights of Step 1 (perspective weight times the local raw weight, where the local raw weight is the sum of the experts' local weights divided by the number of experts) rather than from a separate local-weight multiplication.
9. Desirability and composite scoring
Importance weights say how much a criterion matters. Desirability says how good its performance needs to be. It comes from multi-attribute utility theory and is collected with a short questionnaire per criterion, giving a value curve from 0 to 1 (higher is better; a maximize function was used in the dissertation). The repository includes a Desirability Survey Questions Ranking Guide (PDF) and a workbook sheet that averages the panelists' selected D scores for each criterion.
Composite score steps:
1.	D Score: the average of the experts' desirability scores for the criterion.
2.	Local Weight Raw: the experts' local weight for the criterion.
3.	Perspective Weight: the perspective's average score from the pairwise model.
4.	Global Weight: Perspective Weight times Local Weight Raw.
5.	Criterion Score: D Score times Global Weight.
6.	Composite Score: the sum of all criterion scores.
\text{Composite} = \sum_{c} D_c \times (PW_{p(c)} \times LW_c)
A composite can be computed for different cases (for example different organization types) using the same weights and each case's own D scores.
10. Sensitivity analysis
Sensitivity analysis asks how a composite changes if the experts' perspective priorities were different.
•	Scenario design: one scenario per perspective. In each, that perspective is given a high weight and the others a low weight (the dissertation's method used 0.96 for the boosted perspective and 0.01 for each of the others; these are method settings).
•	Calculation in the repository's Step 2 workbook: each criterion's global weight is multiplied by its perspective's scenario score (the scores are shifted by 1 so the multiplication stays positive, as the sheet header says), the results are re-normalized so they sum to 1, each is multiplied by its D score, and the products are summed into a scenario composite.
•	Reported value: the change of each scenario composite against the baseline composite.
•	Use: it shows which perspectives the composite is most exposed to if the expert average turns out wrong, and checks whether conclusions are robust.
11. Computation and tooling
What the CSAHP repository provides (workbook names as listed there):
•	Step 1 (model setup and single user output): templates and a model-entry sheet; one sheet each for the perspectives, criteria and alternatives comparisons; an import sheet to paste survey results; the grid and decimal conversion; HDM, AHP and CSAHP consistency sheets; density and focus sheets; a desirability export; an individual summary report; and a raw model. The README says 4, 5, 6, 7 and 11 variables are built and tested, and that 3, 8, 9 or 10 variables must be built by working back from the 11-variable layout.
•	Step 2 (desirability and sensitivity, all users final model): D ranges from experts, model results import, composite scoring, sensitivity and a final criteria sheet.
•	Step 3 (alternatives): a single-user workbook with panel worksheets.
•	Demonstrations: an AHP rank and sum mismatch example, and a constant sum 100 scale workbook that shows the misranking problem being overcome.
•	Supporting files: a Google Form export of the survey, the desirability ranking guide, and a references workbook.
What was built in this thread:
•	HDM workbooks: full live workbooks for 4 to 8 variables, and compact workbooks for 8 to 12 variables whose permutation results come from a script.
•	hdm_calc.py: reads the inputs from a workbook or CSV, prints average scores, standard deviations, HDM inconsistency, the RSV value and its table bracket, writes a CSV, and can update a compact workbook. It handles 12 variables in a few minutes on one core.
•	Web form: constant sum input (whole numbers 1 to 99), 4 to 12 variables per set, perspectives and per-perspective criteria, cumulative tables with respondent IDs, a Global weights table and a hierarchy chart.
•	Not in the thread's tools: the 1 to 10 grid, AHP consistency ratio, CSAHP and density scores, focus charts, desirability, composite scoring, sensitivity and alternatives. Those exist only in the repository workbooks.
12. Panel design and validation
•	Panels: group experts by role or organization type so differences can be compared and bias from grouping can be seen. Screen experts for involvement and familiarity with the subject, and brief them on the process before they answer.
•	Structure validation: a validation panel reviews the perspectives and criteria. A criterion is kept if a set share of the panel accepts it (the dissertation used two thirds, or 66.6%).
•	Reliability: the 0.10 inconsistency limit is related to a Cronbach's alpha style rule of thumb, treating about 90% consistency as excellent data.
•	Criterion validity and content validity are assessed by comparing the model with independent expert or interview input.
13. Limitations and cautions
•	Results reflect the experts consulted, so selection, non-response and self-report bias must be managed by balancing panels.
•	HDM does not model flows of information between items (ANP can), and constraints are only approximated through desirability and sensitivity.
•	Factor analysis needs a sample far larger than an expert panel.
•	Computation grows with n factorial, which caps spreadsheet use at about nine items per group.
•	A coarse 1 to 10 scale limits expressiveness, and its reciprocal grid depends on the specific adjustments described in 4.4.
•	The thread's web form keeps data only while the page is open and has no export.
•	The RSV table covers 3 to 12 items.
•	A label error exists in the supplied 5-variable workbook: Permutations cell J3 reads p3 - i3:i4 but should read p6 - i3:i5. It affects permutation 2 and slightly changes the 5-variable results.
14. Recommended procedure
1.	Define the mission, perspectives and criteria. Keep each group to nine items or fewer.
2.	Validate the structure with a panel.
3.	Recruit and brief experts, with balanced panels.
4.	Collect constant sum pairwise comparisons (100 points, or 10 points in the CSAHP workbooks) for every pair.
5.	For each expert, compute weights and the consistency checks (HDM inconsistency, and where used the AHP ratio and CSAHP score). Ask anyone above 0.10 to retake, and remove them if they remain above.
6.	Review density to see whether the expert differentiated among items.
7.	Build the cumulative tables and check group disagreement with a definition agreed in advance.
8.	Compute perspective, local and global weights.
9.	Optionally collect desirability scores, compute composites, run sensitivity scenarios and score alternatives.
10.	Report strengths, weaknesses and recommended actions from the highest and lowest scores.
15. Items to confirm
•	Which Disagreement definition the web form should use (dissertation definition or average of Inconsistency values).
•	Whether to add the CSAHP checks (AHP consistency ratio, CSAHP score, density) and a 1 to 10 input mode to the web form.
•	Whether to add desirability, composite scoring and sensitivity scenarios to the form.
•	Whether to correct the label in the 5-variable workbook.
•	Details of the 1 to 10 grid, the density formula and the CSAHP score are summarized from the repository README and workbook formulas. They were read, not recalculated, and the repository's HDM folder was not opened. Please check them against your own notes.
16. Glossary
•	Constant sum: a judgment in which a fixed total is split between two items.
•	Perspective: an affinity category that groups criteria and has its own weight.
•	Criterion: an item compared with others inside its perspective.
•	Local weight: a criterion's weight within its perspective.
•	Global weight: perspective value times local weight.
•	Permutation (orientation): one ordering of the items. HDM evaluates all of them.
•	Normalization: dividing an ordering's raw weights by their sum so they total 1.
•	HDM inconsistency: the mean of item standard deviations across permutations for one expert.
•	RSV: Root Sum of Variance, an alternative inconsistency measure read from a lookup table.
•	CI, RI, CR: AHP consistency index, random index and consistency ratio.
•	CSAHP: the constant sum AHP and HDM hybrid in the repository.
•	Density: the share of neutral cells in the comparison matrix. A high value means little differentiation.
•	Disagreement: a group-level spread measure across experts.
•	D score (desirability): how strongly a performance level is preferred, from 0 to 1.
•	Composite score: the sum of desirability-weighted global weights.
